  "conclusions": [
    "The sealed report is supported: this is a functional pane-ownership/local-layout correction with lower observed pooled p95 and higher observed mean in every pair. It does not present serial one-host campaigns as a controlled causal speedup.",
    "All original full-suite Unix-socket failures remain exit1; exact affected-package retries pass. Before-regression and unadopted experiment receipts remain historical and are not attributed to final source.",
    "The unchanged p95<=4ms/p99<=6ms targets fail in every final process. The report correctly keeps P6 performance, memory/endurance, hardware, human accessibility and public release gates open.",
    "The producer omits raw machine.json from its allowlisted archive traversal and recursively redacts serial/UUID keys, including JSON-encoded stdout, before sealing metadata. Independent verification did not read the raw file."
  ],
  "limitations": [
    "CPU and opt-in GPU labels identify requested scene-renderer modes, not proof that every source took the GPU path or a measurement of physical GPU/compositor completion.",
    "UI-work excludes queued delay, acquire wait and PresentCallNS; API/presentation timing remains separately measured. No native CPU/GPU allocation or framework-only Go allocation attribution is established.",
    "Four serial campaigns on one host do not eliminate host scheduling/thermal/order effects. No causal speedup or wider hardware result is inferred.",
    "Selected source/binary bindings are not the entire compiler/dependency closure. The complete prepared bytes were rehashed, but whole SDK/release/legal closure was outside this evidence review.",
    "Native selector AX checks do not qualify physical pointer interaction, human VoiceOver, real IME candidate panels, endurance or full Mac completion.",
    "Raw serial/UUID-bearing metadata was deliberately unread; its retained opaque hash is not independently recomputed from the forbidden input."
  ],
